## Step 1 — Resolve the PR

PRs live in `{{ repo_org }}/{{ custom_repo_name }}`, base `{{ odoo_version }}`.

```bash
gh pr view <PR> -R {{ repo_org }}/{{ custom_repo_name }} --json number,title,body,url,state,mergedAt
gh api repos/{{ repo_org }}/{{ custom_repo_name }}/pulls/<PR>/files --paginate --jq '.[] | .status + "\t" + .filename'
```

- `state` **must be `MERGED`** — else stop; this skill deploys merged work only.
- **modules** = top-level dir of each changed path that owns a `__manifest__.py`
  (drop repo-root files like linter configs, `README*`, `.github/...`).
- **new module** = `<module>/__manifest__.py` with status `added` → install with
  `-i`, not `-u`. Mixed → combine flags (`-i new_mod -u existing_mod`).
- **added/removed module** (`added`/`removed` on a `__manifest__.py`) → the
  `--link-addons` step is required this deploy. (Removing from code does not
  uninstall from the DB — flag that as a separate manual step.)
{% if tickets_mcp == 'odoo' %}
- **task key(s)** = the `{{ ticket_prefix }}-xxxx` key(s) in the PR title / body / branch —
  Step 8 posts the deploy note to them.
{% endif %}
- **exclusive-lock migration?** — grep the merged migration scripts for
  statements needing an AccessExclusiveLock:

  ```bash
  # MC^1..MC, never `git show $MC`: on a merge commit git show prints the
  # combined diff, which is empty here, so the grep would pass every PR.
  git -C "${{ project_dir_var }}/src/{{ custom_repo_name }}" diff "$MC^1..$MC" -- '*/migrations/*' \
    | grep -inE '^\+.*(DROP TABLE|DROP COLUMN|ALTER TABLE|TRUNCATE|LOCK TABLE)'
  ```

  Any hit → this deploy runs **Step 2b (service stopped)** instead of Step 2.
- **blast radius** — classify the merged diff (`deploy-checks` skill; extend
  `BLAST_HIGH_MODELS` with the models that carry this business's critical
  documents):

  ```bash
  MC=$(gh pr view <PR> -R {{ repo_org }}/{{ custom_repo_name }} --json mergeCommit --jq .mergeCommit.oid)
  git -C "${{ project_dir_var }}/src/{{ custom_repo_name }}" fetch origin
  python3 "${{ project_dir_var }}/.claude/skills/deploy-checks/scripts/blast-radius.py" \
    --repo "${{ project_dir_var }}/src/{{ custom_repo_name }}" --range "$MC^1..$MC"
  ```

  Capture the final `BLAST_RADIUS=<tier>` — it drives Step 6.5/Step 9: **high**
  → invariant check at T+0 and again at T+60; **medium** → T+0 only; **low** →
  skip the checks.

`-u`/`-i` is **always** run for affected modules — Python changes can alter DB
structure (new fields), so an update is always needed; never skip it.

**Derive the module list from the pull, not from the PR.** `git pull origin
{{ odoo_version }}` fast-forwards the whole branch, so everything merged since prod's last
deploy comes along and goes live on the restart — including PRs nobody asked you
to deploy, which then never get their own `-u`. List what is actually landing:

```bash
PROD_HEAD=$(ssh -F "${{ project_dir_var }}/.ssh/config" prod 'git -C {{ prod_remote_project_dir }}/src/{{ custom_repo_name }} rev-parse HEAD')
git -C "${{ project_dir_var }}/src/{{ custom_repo_name }}" fetch origin
git -C "${{ project_dir_var }}/src/{{ custom_repo_name }}" diff --name-only "$PROD_HEAD..origin/{{ odoo_version }}" | cut -d/ -f1 | sort -u
```

Every module in that list belongs in `<modules>`; name the extra PRs in Step 3
so the approval covers them.

## Step 2 — Run the deploy script (order B: update then restart)

**One named script, never the inline form** — see `scripts/README.md` for why,
and for the permission-rule shapes.

```bash
bash "${{ project_dir_var }}/.claude/skills/deploy/scripts/prod-deploy-modules.sh" <{% if test_ssh_host %}prod|test{% else %}prod{% endif %}> <-u|-i> <modules> [--link-addons]
```

- `<modules>` comma-separated, no spaces. `--link-addons` **only** when a module
  was added or removed.
- Prints the whole remote log, then `DEPLOY_EXIT=<n>`, and exits non-zero when
  the update failed, so a broken deploy cannot read as success.
- It runs exactly this on the box (the live service keeps serving during
  `-u --stop-after-init`; the final `restart` brings it up on the new code):

```sh
set -euo pipefail
export PATH="$HOME/{{ nix_profile_rel }}/bin:$PATH"   # non-interactive SSH lacks the nix PATH; bare `python` = exit 127
cd {{ prod_remote_project_dir }}/src/{{ custom_repo_name }}
git pull origin {{ odoo_version }}
cd {{ prod_remote_project_dir }} && {{ prod_link_addons_cmd }}          # --link-addons only
python {{ prod_remote_project_dir }}/src/odoo/odoo-bin -c {{ prod_remote_odoo_conf }} --workers 0 -d {{ prod_db_name }} <-u|-i> <modules> --stop-after-init --logfile=/dev/stdout
systemctl --user restart odoo{{ service_suffix }}.service
```

`logfile=/dev/stdout` makes the box print `ERROR: couldn't create the logfile
directory` and fall back to stdout — that line is not a failure.

## Step 2b — Exclusive-lock migrations: stop the service first

Use this instead of Step 2 whenever Step 1 found `DROP TABLE` / `DROP COLUMN` /
`ALTER TABLE` / `TRUNCATE` / `LOCK TABLE` in a migration.

**Why.** Those statements need an AccessExclusiveLock while a live worker holds
an AccessShareLock on a related relation, and Postgres resolves it by killing
the migration with `DeadlockDetected`. Odoo commits **per module**, so the
failure does not roll the deploy back to the start: the earlier modules stay
migrated while the running service keeps the OLD registry in memory — a table
already dropped under a service that still has the model, so the next write to
it raises. There is no "retry and hope"; a busy service will collide again.

The service is down for the whole update (minutes, not seconds){% if status_mcp == 'teams' %}, so say so in
the Step-4 START message{% endif %}. Three **separate** SSH calls, and the update runs
detached — a dropped SSH connection must never leave prod stopped:

```bash
# git pull first, service still up
ssh -A -F "${{ project_dir_var }}/.ssh/config" prod 'set -euo pipefail
export PATH="$HOME/{{ nix_profile_rel }}/bin:$PATH"
cd {{ prod_remote_project_dir }}/src/{{ custom_repo_name }} && git pull origin {{ odoo_version }}'

ssh -A -F "${{ project_dir_var }}/.ssh/config" prod 'systemctl --user stop odoo{{ service_suffix }}.service'

ssh -A -F "${{ project_dir_var }}/.ssh/config" prod 'export PATH="$HOME/{{ nix_profile_rel }}/bin:$PATH"; rm -f /tmp/deploy-pr<PR>.log; setsid nohup bash -c "python {{ prod_remote_project_dir }}/src/odoo/odoo-bin -c {{ prod_remote_odoo_conf }} --workers 0 -d {{ prod_db_name }} -u <modules> --stop-after-init --logfile=/tmp/deploy-pr<PR>.log; echo RETRY_EXIT=\$? >> /tmp/deploy-pr<PR>.log" > /dev/null 2>&1 < /dev/null & echo LAUNCHED'

# poll until the marker lands, then read the result
until ssh -A -F "${{ project_dir_var }}/.ssh/config" prod 'grep -q "RETRY_EXIT=" /tmp/deploy-pr<PR>.log 2>/dev/null'; do sleep 20; done
ssh -A -F "${{ project_dir_var }}/.ssh/config" prod 'grep -aE "RETRY_EXIT=|Modules loaded" /tmp/deploy-pr<PR>.log; grep -acE " (ERROR|CRITICAL) " /tmp/deploy-pr<PR>.log'

ssh -A -F "${{ project_dir_var }}/.ssh/config" prod 'systemctl --user start odoo{{ service_suffix }}.service'
```

`RETRY_EXIT=0` + `Modules loaded.` is the success pair. **Non-zero → prod is
down and half-migrated**: do not start the service on old code, fix forward or
restore, and tell the user immediately.

Odoo drops a removed model's table only while the model is still in the
registry, so a retirement PR must drop it in its own migration — expect these
statements whenever a model or field goes away.

