## Step 5 — Run the deploy

Capture the deploy timestamp first — it is the invariant-check window start:

```bash
DEPLOY_TS=$(date -u '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')
```

Then run the Step-2 script, capturing full output to a file — the log is long
and the Step-7 `ERROR`/`CRITICAL` scan needs all of it, not a truncated tail:

```bash
bash "${{ project_dir_var }}/.claude/skills/deploy/scripts/prod-deploy-modules.sh" prod -u <modules> > /tmp/deploy-pr<PR>.log 2>&1; echo "EXIT=$?"
tail -n 30 /tmp/deploy-pr<PR>.log
grep -cE " (ERROR|CRITICAL) " /tmp/deploy-pr<PR>.log
```

On a **Step-2b** deploy run that sequence instead; its success pair is
`RETRY_EXIT=0` + `Modules loaded.` in the remote log, and the rules below read
the same.

`set -euo pipefail` stops at the first failing step, so a non-zero exit is a
real failure.

- **Non-zero exit** -> go straight to Step 7 (failure).{% if use_queue_job %} Do **not** requeue.{% endif +%}
  Do **not** post success.
{% if use_queue_job %}

## Step 6 — Requeue stuck queue jobs (only after a clean deploy)

After the restart, jobs left in `started` are orphaned (runner died on restart)
and `enqueued` ones may be stranded. Requeue to `pending` automatically (same
effect as the OCA "Requeue" button) — **separate SSH call**, so a requeue hiccup
never retro-fails a good deploy:

```bash
bash "${{ project_dir_var }}/.claude/skills/deploy/scripts/prod-requeue-jobs.sh" prod
```

- Read the `REQUEUED <n>` line for the count. No report needed — just do it. The
  script exits non-zero if that marker is missing, so a silent no-op cannot read
  as success; the `requeue()`-missing fallback and the mandatory `--no-http`
  (the live service holds the HTTP port) are inside it.
- If this step errors, the deploy still succeeded — report success and note the
  requeue problem separately.
{% endif %}

## Step 6.5 — Invariant check T+0 (blast radius medium/high)

Read-only check that the live DB still holds its invariants (`deploy-checks`
skill; the script streams over stdin, nothing is copied to prod). Skip on
`BLAST_RADIUS=low`.

If the project has a **write step the check depends on** — refreshing a
reporting table whose cron will not fire inside the window — run it first, from
`scripts/` (see `scripts/README.md`, Project write steps), and say in Step 3
that it writes.

```bash
CHECKS="${{ project_dir_var }}/.claude/skills/deploy-checks/scripts"
cat "$CHECKS/invariant-check.py" "$CHECKS/invariant_local.py" | \
  ssh -A -F "${{ project_dir_var }}/.ssh/config" prod 'export PATH="$HOME/{{ nix_profile_rel }}/bin:$PATH"; INV_SINCE="<DEPLOY_TS>" INV_TIER="<BLAST_RADIUS>" python {{ prod_remote_project_dir }}/src/odoo/odoo-bin shell -c {{ prod_remote_odoo_conf }} -d {{ prod_db_name }} --no-http'
```

- Substitute `<DEPLOY_TS>` with the Step-5 value and `<BLAST_RADIUS>` with the
  Step-1 tier when assembling the command — local env vars don't cross the ssh
  boundary.
- A generic check that is permanently wrong for this project goes in
  `INV_SKIP=<name>` **with the reason written down here**, not silently dropped;
  its replacement belongs in `invariant_local.py`.
- `INV_TIER` gates the deep checks: `high` runs them, `medium`/`low` print
  `SKIP`. Unset = high, so a manual health check never runs a silently narrower
  set.
- The deploy itself already succeeded — Step 7 posts its truthful result either
  way.
{% if status_mcp == 'teams' %}  Any `: FAIL` line → additionally post right after the Step-7
  message:
  ```
  ⚠️ **Post-deploy invariant FAILED — <PR title>**
  <the FAIL lines verbatim>
  Investigating.
  ```
  …and show the user the full output. `: WARN` lines → user only, not the chat.
{% else %}  Show the user every `: FAIL` line verbatim, and the full output.
{% endif %}
- T+0 mostly proves the plumbing and catches restart fallout (stuck crons,
  failed jobs); the data window is still empty — T+60 is the real check.

### Project deep checks

Document each `invariant_local.py` deep check here as the project adds them:
what it reconciles, which UI tool opens the same numbers, and what its normal
background rate is. Two things make a deep check readable at 2 a.m.: a link
straight to the tool, and a stated baseline — a check that counts findings on a
database with chronic breakage must grade against the equal-length window
*before* the deploy, or it fires on every deploy and gets ignored.

A cron-fed back-end legitimately reports "not re-evaluated yet" at T+0; that is
expected there and a finding at T+60.
